1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:05,050
Welcome to Education Session 1: account basics and costs.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:10,450
In this session, we'll look at the numbers on your trading account, and what each one means.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,480
Balance, profit and loss, margin, free margin, leverage, and swaps.

4
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:19,580
Let's start with your balance.

5
00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:28,760
Your balance is the money in your account from closed trades: your deposits, plus or minus every closed trade, minus any withdrawals.

6
00:00:29,510 --> 00:00:32,330
For example, you deposit $10,000.

7
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:39,180
You close a trade with a $500 profit. Your balance is now $10,500.

8
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:43,230
Open trades don't change your balance until they are closed.

9
00:00:45,930 --> 00:00:48,950
So what is your account worth right now? That's your equity.

10
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:54,360
Equity is your balance, plus or minus the profit or loss of your open trades.

11
00:00:55,110 --> 00:01:00,370
If an open trade is $300 down, your equity is $10,200.

12
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:08,920
So you deposited $10,000, your balance is $10,500, and your account is worth $10,200 right now.

13
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:14,530
Next, profit and loss, often called P&L.

14
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:22,450
While a trade is open, its profit or loss is floating, or unrealized. It changes every time the price moves.

15
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,630
When you close the trade, the result becomes closed, or realized, and it's added to your balance.

16
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:35,760
Example: you buy 1 lot of EUR/USD. On this pair, one pip is worth about $10 per lot.

17
00:01:36,510 --> 00:01:41,210
If the price rises 50 pips, you have $500 of floating profit.

18
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:47,320
Close the trade, and that $500 becomes realized, and moves into your balance.

19
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:54,140
Your overall performance is your realized results, plus whatever is still floating on open trades.

20
00:01:56,830 --> 00:01:57,750
Now, margin.

21
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:04,190
Margin is the part of your money the broker sets aside as a deposit, to keep a trade open.

22
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:07,990
It isn't a fee. It's released when the trade is closed.

23
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:13,500
It's required because a leveraged trade controls much more value than you put in.

24
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:17,620
Margin is the trade value, divided by your leverage.

25
00:02:18,370 --> 00:02:31,020
1 lot of EUR/USD at 1.1000 is worth about $110,000. With 1:100 leverage, the margin is $1,100.

26
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:38,340
Trade size and margin move together: half a lot needs half the margin, and two lots need twice as much.

27
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:45,340
Free margin is what's left to open new trades, or to absorb losses.

28
00:02:46,090 --> 00:02:49,290
Free margin equals equity, minus used margin.

29
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:58,480
With equity of $10,200 and $1,100 of used margin, your free margin is $9,100.

30
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:03,830
If your open trades lose money, equity falls, and free margin falls with it.

31
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:10,460
Brokers also watch your margin level: equity divided by used margin, as a percentage.

32
00:03:11,210 --> 00:03:21,480
At the margin call level you get a warning. At the stop out level, the platform starts closing trades automatically. These levels differ by broker, so check yours.

33
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:27,070
That's why keeping plenty of free margin matters. It gives your trades room to breathe.

34
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:31,240
That brings us to leverage.

35
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:36,820
Leverage lets you control a large position with a smaller amount of your own money.

36
00:03:37,570 --> 00:03:44,380
With 1:100 leverage, $1,000 of margin controls a $100,000 position.

37
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:49,260
The benefit: you need less capital for each trade, and keep more of it free.

38
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:55,250
The risk: profit and loss are calculated on the full position, not on your margin.

39
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,490
On a $100,000 position, a 1% move is $1,000. That's the whole margin.

40
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:08,800
Leverage doesn't change how far the price moves. It changes how exposed you are to that move.

41
00:04:09,550 --> 00:04:14,960
So size each trade by the risk you can accept, not by the maximum leverage available.

42
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:18,740
Finally, swaps.

43
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:26,330
A swap is an overnight charge, or sometimes a credit, for keeping a position open past the daily rollover.

44
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:36,740
For currencies, it mainly reflects the interest-rate difference between the two currencies, plus a broker fee. For other instruments, it reflects a financing rate.

45
00:04:37,490 --> 00:04:44,350
Each instrument has one swap rate for buying, and one for selling. You'll find them in the instrument's specification.

46
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:50,770
A common calculation: lots, times the swap rate in points, times the value of one point.

47
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:58,390
For example: 1 lot, a swap of minus 7 points, at $1 per point, costs $7 per night.

48
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:05,480
Once a week, usually on Wednesday for currencies, the swap is charged three times, to cover the weekend.

49
00:05:06,230 --> 00:05:10,120
Swaps add up on trades held for weeks, so include them in your costs.

50
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:13,690
Let's recap.

51
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:19,410
Balance is your money from closed trades. Equity is what your account is worth right now.

52
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:24,060
Floating profit or loss becomes realized when you close the trade.

53
00:05:24,810 --> 00:05:31,990
Margin is a deposit, free margin is your room to trade, and leverage magnifies both gains and losses.

54
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:39,620
Swaps are overnight costs or credits. Together with the spread, and any commission, they make up your trading costs.

55
00:05:40,370 --> 00:05:45,190
In Session 2, we'll look at the orders that help you plan and protect every trade.
